WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:09.534
Four-year-old Alexandre Dumas carried his father's gun up the stairs to the attic.

2
00:00:09.535 --> 00:00:17.413
He had been told that God had taken his father to heaven, so the boy was determined to kill God in revenge for this injustice.

3
00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:25.859
The youngster's boldness signaled the man he would become—a man of action, who went to war and fought duels to defend his honor.

4
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.526
He was also a man who loved life and was always surrounded by friends.

5
00:00:30.527 --> 00:00:38.911
He was a master storyteller—able to weave adventure into stories that still delight audiences and readers around the world.

6
00:00:38.912 --> 00:00:47.628
Alexandre Dumas was born on July 24, 1802, in the village of Villers-Cotterêts, near Paris, France.

7
00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:53.721
His father, the son of a marquis and of a black slave, was a general in Napoleon's army.

8
00:00:53.722 --> 00:01:01.833
When Napoleon refused to pay the general's pension because Alexandre's father had criticized him, the family became very poor.

9
00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:08.324
At the general's death, the poverty worsened, and Alexandre's mother could not afford schooling for her son.

10
00:01:08.325 --> 00:01:15.380
But Alexandre experienced a youth rich in adventure—playing, hunting, and fishing with other young scamps.

11
00:01:15.381 --> 00:01:21.428
When he finally went to school at age ten, he quickly earned the respect of his peers by fighting a bully.

12
00:01:21.429 --> 00:01:25.040
He learned little but developed beautiful penmanship.

13
00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:38.420
As Alexandre grew up, he always tried to live up to the stories of his father, a great warrior who single-handedly defended a bridge against Austrian troops and a man who had the nerve to stand up to Napoleon.

14
00:01:38.421 --> 00:01:45.141
In 1823, Dumas moved to Paris, the center of literature and culture in France.

15
00:01:45.142 --> 00:01:55.231
He used his excellent penmanship to obtain a job copying documents for the Duke of Orléans, and in his spare time, he wrote plays and read voraciously.

16
00:01:55.232 --> 00:02:03.942
Confident of his talent as a writer, he said, "I will live by my penmanship now, but the time will come when I shall live by my pen."

17
00:02:03.943 --> 00:02:13.602
He wrote several plays with a friend—two comedies were presented in Paris theaters—but the direction of his writing changed when he saw Shakespeare's Hamlet.

18
00:02:13.603 --> 00:02:24.314
He liked the English playwright's use of history and said he felt like "someone blind from birth whose sight is suddenly restored and who discovers a whole new world."

19
00:02:24.315 --> 00:02:39.360
The inspiration for his next play, Henry III and His Court, came from a real event: a nobleman, finding his wife unfaithful to him, gave her the choice to either kill herself or arrange a meeting with her lover so he can be punished.

20
00:02:39.361 --> 00:02:49.252
Dumas developed this incident into a play about the politics of France in the 1500s—full of snappy dialogue, daring heroes, and hateful villains.

21
00:02:49.253 --> 00:02:53.748
Produced in 1829, the show was a huge success.

22
00:02:53.749 --> 00:03:03.394
Dumas became known for his ability to make historical events and characters more exciting and dramatic using his own experiences and vivid imagination.

23
00:03:03.395 --> 00:03:12.849
During the 1830s, newspapers in France printed stories by chapters in successive issues—known as serial novels—to attract readers.

24
00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:21.894
Between 1844 and 1846, Dumas wrote The Three Musketeers and The Count of Monte Cristo in serial form.

25
00:03:21.895 --> 00:03:28.934
Packed with the adventures and rapid-fire dialogue of his plays, these two novels are his most famous works.

26
00:03:28.935 --> 00:03:39.429
The Three Musketeers gave the world the heroes D'Artagnan, Athos, Porthos, and Aramis, as well as the quote "One for all and all for one."

27
00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:48.304
Pitting Dumas' heroes against the evil Cardinal Richelieu in defense of the queen of France, the book brought seventeenth-century France to life.

28
00:03:48.305 --> 00:03:58.345
In The Count of Monte Cristo, the hero, Edmond Dantès, is falsely accused by a man who wants to marry Dantès' fiancée and is sent to prison.

29
00:03:58.346 --> 00:04:02.744
There, he learns of a treasure on the island of Monte Cristo.

30
00:04:02.745 --> 00:04:11.008
After a daring escape, he uses the treasure to reward his benefactors and punish those responsible for his misfortunes.

31
00:04:11.009 --> 00:04:22.566
The theme in both books—righting wrongs and obtaining revenge against abuses by the powerful—echoes the writer's feelings toward the abuses his father suffered at the hands of Napoleon.

32
00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:31.504
Dumas was a prolific writer. During the 1830s, at least one of his plays was produced each year; in some years, four or five.

33
00:04:31.506 --> 00:04:37.211
In one year, 1844, he completed four novels and began a fifth.

34
00:04:37.212 --> 00:04:42.988
Over his lifetime, he wrote about three hundred literary works, including a cookbook.

35
00:04:42.989 --> 00:04:52.922
He worked ten to fourteen hours a day, but was criticized for using other writers to conduct research, develop outlines, and sometimes create first drafts.

36
00:04:52.923 --> 00:04:59.629
This infuriated Dumas. He even dueled with a collaborator who accused him of stealing his work.

37
00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:10.084
Dumas' supporters point out that he was able to write a novel or a play in days without help and that none of his collaborators ever wrote a successful work alone.

38
00:05:10.085 --> 00:05:18.959
It took Dumas' genius—his special ability to combine action, dialogue, and suspense—to turn history into exciting novels.

39
00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:26.609
Dumas had to work hard because he needed money for his frequent trips abroad, involvement in politics, and rich social life.

40
00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:37.112
Dumas lived outside France for many years; his journeys became the source for popular travel books, in which he wove anecdotes with facts about the various regions he visited.

41
00:05:37.113 --> 00:05:45.423
Throughout his life, he participated in political activities to help the cause of democracy and to try to live up to his father's memory.

42
00:05:45.424 --> 00:05:55.481
For instance, in 1830, he joined the fight against France's king, Charles X, and seized more than a ton of explosives for the uprising.

43
00:05:55.482 --> 00:06:05.474
In 1860, with his schooner, the Emma, he transported guns for the Italian fight for independence and sailed from port to port, inciting revolt.

44
00:06:05.475 --> 00:06:09.241
But above all, he lived for those he loved.

45
00:06:09.242 --> 00:06:14.630
He had dozens of mistresses and acknowledged three children, each by a different mother.

46
00:06:14.631 --> 00:06:19.464
His son, also named Alexandre Dumas, became a famous playwright.

47
00:06:19.465 --> 00:06:29.232
(To avoid confusion, the elder Alexandre is referred to as Dumas pere, meaning "father," and the younger, as Dumas fils, meaning "son.")

48
00:06:29.233 --> 00:06:36.231
He supported his mistresses and children and always kept a champagne glass full of money ready to aid friends in need.

49
00:06:36.232 --> 00:06:44.998
And he had many friends; he invited six hundred people to celebrate the completion of his extravagant home, the Château of Monte Cristo!

50
00:06:44.999 --> 00:06:55.389
Unfortunately, even Dumas' substantial income could not keep up with his lavish lifestyle, and he had to sell Monte Cristo at a fraction of its cost.

51
00:06:55.390 --> 00:07:01.540
As the end of his life approached, he was unable to write enough to pay his bills and became destitute.

52
00:07:01.541 --> 00:07:09.916
Dumas' adventures ended on December 5, 1870, when he died of a stroke at his son's home in Puys, France.

53
00:07:09.917 --> 00:07:19.207
Alexandre Dumas pere experienced love, war, and intrigue through his travels and friendships with both commoners and royalty.

54
00:07:19.208 --> 00:07:25.626
He made many fortunes and lost them all in pursuit of a meaningful, adventurous life.

55
00:07:25.627 --> 00:07:32.450
He achieved his goal of becoming worthy of his father's memory, but he wondered whether he would be remembered as a writer.

56
00:07:32.451 --> 00:07:45.113
The answer is clear: over 150 years after his death, his work lives on not only in books but also in films, cartoons, plays, and television programs.

57
00:07:45.114 --> 00:07:54.586
His genius continues to inform audiences and make them laugh and cry, just as his life inspires people to live their own lives to the fullest.
